WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.475 --> 00:00:08.000
INTRODUCTION:

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:10.320
So, the Voltaic benchmarks…

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:15.920
If you are into aim training you have probably&nbsp;
heard this term once or twice. If you haven’t…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:18.240
then why are you watching this video?

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:23.440
Even though everyone pretty much gets the&nbsp;
basics of the Voltaic benchmarks and ranks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:27.280
there is still a lot of confusion on how&nbsp;
to actually use them and how to practice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:31.280
in order to get better at them.
How often should I use them?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:35.760
should I grind them? how do I get better at&nbsp;
specific benchmark? Should I only aim train&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:40.080
with the fundamental playlists or should I use&nbsp;
other playlists as well? And so, on and son on.

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:45.440
The truth is, there isn’t a definitive&nbsp;
answer to these questions, and it mostly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:49.200
comes down to opinion and what YOU are&nbsp;
trying to achieve with aim training.

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:55.280
If you are just trying to get that sweet sweet&nbsp;
street cred and flex with a high Voltaic rank,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:58.640
you would probably approach the benchmarks&nbsp;
in a different manner as someone trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:02.480
to improve their overall aim, or someone&nbsp;
just trying to improve in a specific game.

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:08.640
While all of these goals are perfectly fine and&nbsp;
valid, this video is intended to people that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:13.360
trying to get better aim in fps games in general,&nbsp;
and use the benchmarks either as a way to check on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:17.360
their progress or as an additional motivation to&nbsp;
push them further in their aim training journey.

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:19.840
OBSERVATIONS:

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:23.760
Through Reddit polls and just watching what&nbsp;
people in the aim training community do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:28.080
I’ve found that most people that fall in this&nbsp;
category usually use the benchmarks either once&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:33.760
a week or once a month, with the majority choosing&nbsp;
the former. And they aim train through the week,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:37.360
usually doing the fundamental playlists from&nbsp;
voltaic or with a combination of different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:41.520
playlist they’ve gathered from popular creators&nbsp;
or people that compile them such as PureG,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:44.560
RiddBTW, WestProter, 4bangerkovaaks and many more.

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:49.040
While the fundamental playlists are good&nbsp;
“general” playlists to play since they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:53.280
cover most aiming techniques, I find that after&nbsp;
a beginner level they do not provide the amount&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:56.960
of repetition and challenge required to push&nbsp;
you towards fast and efficient improvement.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:02.160
And since they try to condense every aiming&nbsp;
category in a playlist that lasts roughly an hour,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:05.120
they inevitably suffer from a lack&nbsp;
of scenario variety and repetition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:08.160
times that become essential to your&nbsp;
progress later in your aim journey.

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:14.480
With this I am not saying that you should&nbsp;
never play these routines, but on their own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:18.560
they are probably not enough, or at least not&nbsp;
the most efficient path to improve your aim.

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:24.240
On the opposite end of the spectrum, playlists&nbsp;
made by other creators, could potentially be too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:29.360
specific or harder than what you need right now&nbsp;
in relation to your current skill level. And even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:34.000
though there are amazing playlists and scenarios&nbsp;
made by great creators and aimers out there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:38.144
it could be hard to find the best playlist for you&nbsp;
if you don’t know where to look or even what you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.144 --> 00:02:42.880
are looking for, and you could end up giving&nbsp;
too much of your time and attention to skills&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:47.056
that are secondary or even harming you because you&nbsp;
lack the fundamentals necessary to know what you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.056 --> 00:02:51.760
are trying to improve at, ultimately creating bad&nbsp;
habits that would be hard to get rid of later on.

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:56.560
THE VOLTAIC DAILY IMPROVEMENT METHOD:

00:02:56.560 --> 00:03:02.400
So, what I propose is the following. As you&nbsp;
know, Voltaic divides aiming as a general skill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:08.080
into three main categories; Clicking, Tracking&nbsp;
and Target Switching. And these are separated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:13.200
into 2 subcategories each; dynamic clicking,&nbsp;
static clicking, smooth or precise tracking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:18.080
reactive tracking, speedTS and&nbsp;
evasive TS. And also, with Season 4,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:21.760
for the intermediate and advanced benchmarks,&nbsp;
Voltaic added movement scenarios as well.

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:25.600
With the introduction of this&nbsp;
new season I decided to find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:28.960
an alternative way to go about my aim&nbsp;
training instead of doing the classic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:32.640
fundamental playlist plus a more specific&nbsp;
playlist combo I was doing last season.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:38.160
After some time thinking about it I decided I&nbsp;
wanted to create playlists that worked both as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:42.480
a way to improve my overall aim, but also&nbsp;
as a way to get just enough extra practice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:46.640
to be ready to beat my high scores on the&nbsp;
benchmark scenarios. Because let’s face it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:50.000
beating PB’s is a really great feeling and&nbsp;
a great motivation to keep aim training.

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:56.400
In time, and with the great help of VT MattyOW who&nbsp;
provided amazing feedback on all of the playlists,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:00.880
my friend TenguTerror who I basically used as&nbsp;
a test subject for the last couple of weeks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:05.840
the approval of SilkyCrisp who’s a great&nbsp;
inspiration of mine, and the initial feedback&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:10.320
I got when I started working on these playlists&nbsp;
by RiddBTW and WestProter, I managed to create&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:15.120
7 playlists that I’m really proud of, one for&nbsp;
each aiming technique and correlating to a day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:19.920
of the week designed to properly and fully train&nbsp;
each technique in relation to your Voltaic rank.

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:23.040
These playlists, whose Kovaaks sharecodes&nbsp;
are in the description by the way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:28.560
are divided into Novice, and Intermediate and as&nbsp;
said before, are titled after an aiming technique&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:34.320
and day of the week. So, for example, if you&nbsp;
are platinum you would download the intermediate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:38.800
playlists (by the way, to do this, you must go&nbsp;
search the code in the description and copy it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:42.000
then in kovaaks head to online playlists&nbsp;
and at the bottom you must paste&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:45.520
it and press enter) and every Monday you&nbsp;
would play the Dynamic clicking playlist.

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:52.080
In this playlist you will find different bot types&nbsp;
and scenarios to help you on your overall dynamic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:56.160
clicking technique, but also harder versions&nbsp;
of the dynamic clicking intermediate benchmarks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.160 --> 00:04:59.280
and the benchmarks themselves. So technically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:03.440
every time you fully run each one of these&nbsp;
playlists, you will also be benchmarking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:09.600
that specific technique, in this example VT Pasu&nbsp;
Rasp Intermediate and VT Bounceshot Intermediate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:12.800
and the same goes for every other&nbsp;
aiming technique and benchmark scenario.

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:16.560
I think this way to go about&nbsp;
benchmarking has many benefits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:19.040
over the established practice&nbsp;
of benchmarking once a week,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:24.000
the main reasons you would want to do this,&nbsp;
in no special order, are the following:

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:26.640
1.- You are not “missing” training days.

00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:33.200
There are 16 benchmark scenarios. All lasting at&nbsp;
least a minute. If you were to run all of them at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:39.280
least five times each, this would be a total of&nbsp;
80 minutes. 80 minutes is quite a lot of time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:43.120
and this is even without considering the time&nbsp;
you probably waste by resetting runs of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:48.320
benchmarks. Or all the time you spend getting&nbsp;
stuck running one benchmark 20 times in a row.

00:05:48.320 --> 00:05:52.640
From what I’ve seen from posts in the aiming&nbsp;
community, most people have a maximum of 1&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.640 --> 00:05:58.960
hour a day to aim train, some even less. So,&nbsp;
this essentially means that “benchmark day”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.960 --> 00:06:03.280
usually Sundays, are a lost day for aim&nbsp;
training, since you are playing all of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:06.960
benchmarks and trying to get high scores&nbsp;
and not actually focusing on improvement.

00:06:08.080 --> 00:06:12.480
With the playlists I made, you are actually&nbsp;
aim training every day, including Sundays,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.480 --> 00:06:17.200
for around 45 to 50 minutes, and only&nbsp;
spending 10 minutes a day for benchmarking.

00:06:17.200 --> 00:06:20.400
2.- You are benchmarking at your full potential.

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:24.800
Let’s continue with the example of the 80&nbsp;
minutes I calculated on the last point.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:28.960
Assuming you follow the order of the benchmark&nbsp;
spreadsheet and started benchmarking with VT&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:33.840
Pasu Rasp Intermediate, there is no way&nbsp;
that after going all out on the benchmarks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:37.600
trying to beat your high scores you can&nbsp;
perform optimally by the time you get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.600 --> 00:06:42.560
to VT Bounce TS intermediate. You are basically&nbsp;
handicapping yourself by making you really tired&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:44.880
and by the time you get to the&nbsp;
last scenarios you will perform&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:47.840
worse than what you would have if you had&nbsp;
started in the bottom of the spreadsheet.

00:06:49.280 --> 00:06:54.000
Basically, with my method, you are only doing two&nbsp;
different benchmarks per day, and all of the other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:57.840
scenarios you play are meant for you to gradually&nbsp;
improve your aim, without the need for you to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:03.120
grind them with the same intensity as if they&nbsp;
were all benchmarks. Plus, since they are all part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:07.360
of the same aiming technique, you will likely be&nbsp;
readier to face the benchmark scenarios and warmed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.360 --> 00:07:12.880
up to try to beat your high scores, as opposed to&nbsp;
doing all of the benchmarks on the same day, in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.880 --> 00:07:16.960
which you are essentially jumping from technique&nbsp;
to technique without having the time to adjust.

00:07:16.960 --> 00:07:21.840
3.- the benchmarks are comparatively easier&nbsp;
than the harder versions of the scenarios.

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:26.400
This is a well-known practice and one of the&nbsp;
two main things I wanted to achieve by making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:31.600
these playlists. On every one of these playlists&nbsp;
I added a harder version of the benchmark scenario&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:35.680
of the technique you are training. The point of&nbsp;
this is that just before you have to play the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:39.840
benchmark, you play a more demanding version&nbsp;
a couple of times. This will have the effect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.840 --> 00:07:43.280
of making it so that when it is time&nbsp;
to run the actual benchmark scenario,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.280 --> 00:07:47.280
it will feel way easier for you, helping&nbsp;
you break plateaus and get higher scores.

00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:51.680
4.- You can optimize your training&nbsp;
session for the specific aiming technique.

00:07:51.680 --> 00:07:55.360
A very overlooked and often misunderstood&nbsp;
aspect of kovaaks, is that you should try&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.360 --> 00:07:59.360
to optimize it as much as you can in order for&nbsp;
you to train in the most effective way possible.

00:07:59.920 --> 00:08:04.000
There are a lot of posts of people trying&nbsp;
to use their main game’s FOV while training&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:07.520
despite it not being the most optimal way to&nbsp;
run the specific scenario they are playing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:11.600
or people searching for gun skins&nbsp;
to mimic what they see in game even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.600 --> 00:08:15.360
though this results in having half of their&nbsp;
screen covered, and many other examples.

00:08:15.360 --> 00:08:18.800
You have to remember that kovaaks is a&nbsp;
tool meant for you to train your aim,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.800 --> 00:08:22.400
so you should try to avoid anything&nbsp;
that limits your ability to do that.

00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:26.240
So, you may be wondering how does&nbsp;
my method helps you to do that?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.240 --> 00:08:29.920
And the answer is simple: visual themes and FOV.

00:08:29.920 --> 00:08:34.640
It’s no secret that there are visual themes and&nbsp;
FOV settings that work better for certain scenario&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.640 --> 00:08:40.400
types. The most common example is the classic grey&nbsp;
background with black targets for static clicking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.040 --> 00:08:44.160
and FOV higher than 103 for&nbsp;
target switching scenarios.

00:08:44.160 --> 00:08:48.000
If you want to make kovaaks as optimized as&nbsp;
possible you should be using the settings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:51.760
that work better for you and change them&nbsp;
depending on the scenario type you are using.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.760 --> 00:08:55.760
What my playlists offer in this regard,&nbsp;
is that before even starting to play them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.760 --> 00:08:59.520
you can set your preferred visual theme and&nbsp;
FOV and then don’t worry about it for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:03.520
rest of the day since you are training the same&nbsp;
aiming technique in mostly similar scenarios,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.080 --> 00:09:09.040
instead of what you would have to do while playing&nbsp;
the fundamental playlist or in the benchmark day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.040 --> 00:09:11.920
in which you would have to go to your&nbsp;
settings and change the visual theme&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.920 --> 00:09:16.720
and FOV every time you have to play a&nbsp;
different scenario, while this is doable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.720 --> 00:09:21.680
it’s certainly annoying, and easily forgettable,&nbsp;
with my method you are more likely to be using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.680 --> 00:09:25.840
the most optimized setting for you in every aim&nbsp;
training session. (by the way, in the description&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.840 --> 00:09:30.240
of every playlist I give my recommendation of&nbsp;
the FOV and sensitivity you should be using)

00:09:30.240 --> 00:09:34.240
5.- Personally, it worked for me.

00:09:35.280 --> 00:09:40.480
After using these playlists, I have to say that&nbsp;
I have personally seen actual fast improvement.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.280 --> 00:09:45.680
